add two electrons to the left hand

00:13:19.710 --> 00:13:24.839
side, that will now make this entire
left side negative one as well.

00:13:25.365 --> 00:13:29.885
So this will be our half reaction for
the reduction side of the problem.

00:13:30.275 --> 00:13:31.905
Now let's put it all together.

00:13:32.095 --> 00:13:35.545
Our next part is all about
canceling the electrons, right?

00:13:35.545 --> 00:13:40.094
You've never seen a chemical reaction
where the electrons are listed in the

00:13:40.094 --> 00:13:42.284
reaction, so we have to cancel them.

00:13:42.754 --> 00:13:47.455
Now the only way that's possible is
when we multiply these half reactions by

00:13:47.455 --> 00:13:52.075
some kind of factor to get the electrons
to be equal in the top and the bottom.

00:13:53.225 --> 00:13:55.075
First thing is, before you
kind of go anywhere, is

00:13:55.085 --> 00:13:56.315
always double check your work.

00:13:56.875 --> 00:13:59.385
Make sure that there are
electrons on the left and the

00:13:59.385 --> 00:14:00.825
right hand side of the equation.

00:14:00.945 --> 00:14:03.745
If they're on the same side, that
means you did something wrong when

00:14:03.745 --> 00:14:05.554
it came to calculating your charge.

00:14:05.965 --> 00:14:09.344
Um, same thing with hydrogens,
generally they're on the opposite side

00:14:09.345 --> 00:14:11.695
as well, so double check that too.

00:14:12.044 --> 00:14:15.365
So in this case, like you see that
we have 12 on the, on the right, on

00:14:15.365 --> 00:14:16.965
the top one, and then 2 on the left.

00:14:17.395 --> 00:14:19.415
So what we need to do is
we need to make them equal.

00:14:19.425 --> 00:14:22.495
So being that the bottom is a smaller
number, What we're going to do is we're

00:14:22.495 --> 00:14:25.115
going to multiply the bottom by 5.

00:14:25.504 --> 00:14:28.104
So, therefore be 5 times 2 electrons.

00:14:28.494 --> 00:14:30.844
So what we're going to do is
we're going to rewrite the

00:14:30.844 --> 00:14:32.935
bottom times everything by 5.

00:14:33.375 --> 00:14:37.954
So I have 10 electrons, I'll
have 10 protons, then I'm going

00:14:37.954 --> 00:14:42.635
to have 5 OCL minus, right?

00:14:43.199 --> 00:14:49.839
I'm going to have five chlorines, and
then we will also have five waters that

00:14:49.839 --> 00:14:51.319
we're going to have on the problem.

00:14:51.769 --> 00:14:54.270
Okay, so we multiplied everything by five.

00:14:54.910 --> 00:14:59.949
Now, so what that means is we can kind of
get rid of this entire thing on the top.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:02.354
We don't need it anymore,
so we'll get rid of that.

00:15:03.094 --> 00:15:03.454
All right.

00:15:03.454 --> 00:15:06.884
So now what we'll do is we can now put
the two equations next to each other.

00:15:07.474 --> 00:15:10.724
And this is where our next step
is going to come into play.

00:15:10.854 --> 00:15:11.334
Okay.

00:15:11.334 --> 00:15:14.224
So our last step, what we're going to be
doing is we're going to be adding things

00:15:14.224 --> 00:15:16.234
together, almost like a math problem.

00:15:16.645 --> 00:15:19.904
We're going to add together the two
half reactions, and then we're going

00:15:19.904 --> 00:15:25.255
to cancel anything out that's matching
on the left and right hand side.

00:15:25.624 --> 00:15:29.034
So like, for example,
the get canceled, right?

00:15:29.034 --> 00:15:31.755
So we have 10 electrons on
the right, 10 on the left.

00:15:32.064 --> 00:15:35.795
So something like that is going
to end up getting cancelled.

00:15:36.265 --> 00:15:40.214
Now what else is going to get cancelled
is anything else that matches.

00:15:40.625 --> 00:15:44.054
So notice how we added waters
and hydrogens to the mix.

00:15:44.515 --> 00:15:46.115
Those are things that we're looking for.

00:15:46.594 --> 00:15:49.344
So we had five waters on the right.

00:15:49.414 --> 00:15:52.004
We had six on the left hand side.

00:15:52.425 --> 00:15:55.515
So what does that mean is that
we're going to end up reducing that

00:15:55.515 --> 00:15:56.985
coefficient down to one, right?

00:15:57.475 --> 00:15:59.795
So five into six, we'll
have one left over.

00:16:00.435 --> 00:16:01.935
Same thing with the hydrogens, right?

00:16:01.965 --> 00:16:06.485
So I'm going to cancel all of the
ones that are on the left hand side.

00:16:06.975 --> 00:16:10.345
And as a result, we're only going
to have two hydrogens that are

00:16:10.345 --> 00:16:12.405
going to be remaining on the right.

00:16:12.905 --> 00:16:15.905
So now what we're going to do is
we're going to take everything and

00:16:16.055 --> 00:16:18.805
add it together into one big reaction.

00:16:19.205 --> 00:16:22.875
And that's going to be our final answer
is going to be our balanced reaction.

00:16:22.885 --> 00:16:23.345
So right.

00:16:23.345 --> 00:16:23.665
We have.

00:16:24.015 --> 00:16:27.194
One water, and this is the kind of
the part to be careful with, make

00:16:27.194 --> 00:16:28.444
sure you're getting everything.

00:16:28.694 --> 00:16:32.114
It's so easy to actually
miss something, right?

00:16:32.114 --> 00:16:39.365
We have our iodine, then we have our
5 O C L minus, and that's all going

00:16:39.365 --> 00:16:41.365
to be everything that's on the left.

00:16:41.865 --> 00:16:49.165
Now we have our two iodates, 5 O 3 minus,
and that's aqueous, and that's going to

00:16:49.165 --> 00:16:55.155
be added to our five chlorines, and then
the only thing left is our two hydrogens.

00:16:55.610 --> 00:17:00.400
Right, so put them back in their green,
and that shows that we have our acidic

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:02.260
component in the reaction as well.

00:17:02.770 --> 00:17:05.069
And those are going to be aqueous as well.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:08.790
So that is our entire balance process.

00:17:09.360 --> 00:17:10.470
It was very long, right?

00:17:10.480 --> 00:17:11.559
It's a lot of steps.

00:17:11.570 --> 00:17:14.520
So let's take a look and
summarize all what we just did.

00:17:14.690 --> 00:17:16.400
All right, that's our video, right?

00:17:16.410 --> 00:17:19.330
So this is all those different
steps that we had to go through.

00:17:19.680 --> 00:17:22.980
And you could see that it was a lot,
right, especially when we started

00:17:22.980 --> 00:17:27.010
with the following unbalanced
equation and we balanced it.

00:17:27.379 --> 00:17:30.470
So one of the things that I would
really recommend is kind of just, it's

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:34.729
really boils down to just a few steps
is first you write your half reactions,

00:17:35.209 --> 00:17:40.880
then balance it other atoms first,
then oxygen with water, then hydrogen

00:17:41.240 --> 00:17:45.929
with protons, and then balance your
charge electrons and then combine them.

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:47.819
It's very procedural.

00:17:47.860 --> 00:17:49.320
It takes practice with anything.

00:17:49.889 --> 00:17:51.179
But you can do this.

00:17:51.210 --> 00:17:54.520
Um, if you can conquer this,
arguably, this is probably one of the

00:17:54.679 --> 00:17:58.370
hardest things you have to achieve
in general chemistry, one or two.

00:17:58.709 --> 00:18:00.800
That does not involve a lot of math.

00:18:01.189 --> 00:18:03.749
So especially in terms of
balancing, this is probably the

00:18:03.749 --> 00:18:05.590
most difficult balancing process.

00:18:06.009 --> 00:18:09.140
But if you can do these, you can
really handle any type of balancing.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:12.179
Okay, well, thank you
so much for listening.

